<p>The 70th Eurovision Song Contest reaches its final Saturday, May 16, in Vienna, with 25 acts scheduled to perform at the Wiener Stadthalle under tight security. Spain, Ireland, Iceland, the Netherlands and Slovenia are not taking part, objecting to Israel’s participation during the Gaza war. Israel’s entrant, Noam Bettan, qualified for the final after performing “Michelle” in the first semifinal, where some audience members booed and shouted during quieter moments of the song. Pro-Palestinian demonstrations and alternative events were scheduled or held in Vienna during contest week, including actions under the slogan “No Stage for Genocide”. The European Broadcasting Union decided in December 2025 not to block Israel’s 2026 entry, and Bettan is scheduled to perform third in Saturday’s final.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/lig2mMggnqL6P7QiQeyjFBo5FA5UINcZgLs03o5dnPeDi15zzmNtOAbTZymtiG6fSSTMjYt51ESoy2LUC0c6K_tXxHrxB0gbO_RnTlGYrSRN2AGeLi8aYLcZMYHBmoX79s-Nzjatv47zYPar28stXutlCd0eHOt5HTess9I3fnqBw5RA1tM8pNd8jdtW3v4LejL-uZv7sJ8xODmE989PCwlIjG4ijudtjRenIJ8CgfKWRLDDitLwjy0' alt='Noam Bettan, Israel&#x27;s contestant for Eurovision 2026, performs the song &quot;Michelle&quot; during the first semi-final of the contest&#x27;s 70th edition in Vienna, on May 12, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Reduced field: Thirty-five countries are participating in the 2026 contest, which El País reported is the smallest field since 2004.</li><li>Artist pressure: More than 1,000 artists signed a boycott appeal over Israel’s presence. RP Online named Peter Gabriel, Brian Eno and Paul Weller among the signatories.</li><li>Audience scale: AP material carried by ClickOrlando said the five-day contest drew 166 million viewers last year, exceeding Super Bowl viewership in the United States.</li><li>Voting dispute: El País reported suspicions of televoting manipulation; Waikato Times reported that Israel denied rigging the Eurovision vote.</li><li>Branding scrutiny: El País reported that contestants did not walk a red carpet this year; they used a turquoise carpet matching the Pantone shade of Moroccanoil packaging, with the Israeli cosmetics company identified as the festival’s main sponsor.</li></ul><blockquote>It’s not the same watching it alone at home as it is with friends. That’s the only thing that upsets me. - Silvia Díaz</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>European Broadcasting Union: Eurovision director Martin Green wrote that organizers were planning for all scenarios as the contest faced withdrawals and protests over Israel’s participation. (<a href='https://www.expressen.se/noje/musik/landerna-som-bojkottar-eurovision-song-contest-ebus-svar/'>Expressen</a>)</li><li>RTÉ: Ireland’s public broadcaster said taking part would be “unconscionable” given civilian deaths, the humanitarian crisis in Gaza, the killing of journalists and restrictions on international journalists entering the territory. (<a href='https://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/entry/eurovision-every-country-withdrew-israel_uk_6a059ec8e4b0def65bfc0c6e'>HuffPost UK</a>)</li><li>Fans from boycotting countries: Hufvudstadsbladet quoted Henk from the Netherlands saying it was a shame the EBU did not take fans into account, while the outlet’s headline quoted another fan view: “We’re not going to let Israel ruin our party”. (<a href='https://www.hbl.fi/kultur/eurovisionfans-i-wien-vi-tanker-inte-lata-israel-forstora-var-fest/'>Hufvudstadsbladet</a>)</li><li>Young Greens of Austria: The youth Green movement welcomed Bettan and said boycotting Israel’s participation and harassing Israeli artists would not free Palestine and would contribute to an unsafe environment for Jews in Europe. (<a href='https://www.jta.org/2026/05/15/global/amid-eurovision-tensions-israelis-find-allies-in-austrias-youth-green-party-and-a-cafe'>Jewish Telegraphic Agency</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/05/16/eurovision-2026-israel-s